00:00:00,070 --> 00:00:01,779
Welcome to Resgrid Academy.

2
00:00:02,200 --> 00:00:05,900
In this lesson, we will plan coverage and keep dispatch aligned.

3
00:00:06,400 --> 00:00:10,729
A paper duty schedule and a dispatch roster can drift apart, especially

4
00:00:10,729 --> 00:00:12,890
when members sign up or trade shifts.

5
00:00:13,319 --> 00:00:16,489
We will explain the features, the setup decisions, and a

6
00:00:16,489 --> 00:00:18,420
practical way to check the result.

7
00:00:19,080 --> 00:00:24,330
A career department sets up A, B and C shifts, and a volunteer department uses

8
00:00:24,330 --> 00:00:26,540
shift sign-ups for weekend duty crews.

9
00:00:26,999 --> 00:00:31,039
Shifts show who is on duty and can drive dispatch, so calls page the

10
00:00:31,039 --> 00:00:32,789
people who are actually working.

11
00:00:33,230 --> 00:00:36,620
The examples in this series use a fictional department and are teaching

12
00:00:36,620 --> 00:00:38,860
scenarios, not default settings.

13
00:00:39,460 --> 00:00:40,109
Shifts.

14
00:00:40,489 --> 00:00:43,200
Shift schedules, sign-ups and trades.

15
00:00:43,619 --> 00:00:47,019
Create your shifts, assign groups and members, and decide

16
00:00:47,019 --> 00:00:48,880
whether dispatch follows shifts.

17
00:00:49,230 --> 00:00:51,560
Availability also depends on Shifts.

18
00:00:51,970 --> 00:00:54,119
Check access in the target department.

19
00:00:54,710 --> 00:00:55,390
Calendar.

20
00:00:55,759 --> 00:00:59,329
The department calendar for meetings, events and training nights.

21
00:00:59,749 --> 00:01:01,949
Add recurring meetings and training nights.

22
00:01:02,329 --> 00:01:04,739
Availability also depends on Calendar.

23
00:01:05,180 --> 00:01:07,329
Check access in the target department.

24
00:01:07,909 --> 00:01:09,220
New Calendar Event.

25
00:01:09,659 --> 00:01:11,669
Add an event to the department calendar.

26
00:01:12,019 --> 00:01:15,090
Have your rotation or duty schedule, and decide whether

27
00:01:15,090 --> 00:01:16,850
dispatch should follow shifts.

28
00:01:17,180 --> 00:01:19,539
Availability also depends on Calendar.

29
00:01:20,010 --> 00:01:22,180
Check access in the target department.

30
00:01:22,789 --> 00:01:23,209
Workshifts.

31
00:01:23,940 --> 00:01:25,920
Repeating work schedules for staff.

32
00:01:26,309 --> 00:01:29,309
Have your rotation or duty schedule, and decide whether

33
00:01:29,309 --> 00:01:31,000
dispatch should follow shifts.

34
00:01:31,389 --> 00:01:33,790
Availability also depends on Shifts.

35
00:01:34,190 --> 00:01:36,299
Check access in the target department.

36
00:01:36,860 --> 00:01:39,619
For the training scenario, work in this order.

37
00:01:40,110 --> 00:01:40,719
Step 1.

38
00:01:41,229 --> 00:01:45,030
Create an assigned or signup shift with groups and staffing needs.

39
00:01:45,460 --> 00:01:46,210
Step 2.

40
00:01:46,610 --> 00:01:50,099
Have demo members sign up or complete a trade where supported.

41
00:01:50,580 --> 00:01:51,320
Step 3.

42
00:01:51,750 --> 00:01:56,390
Review whether dispatch follows groups or shifts and confirm the calendar time zone.

43
00:01:57,029 --> 00:01:58,560
Now verify the result.

44
00:01:58,990 --> 00:02:01,790
Compare the scheduled roster with the people dispatch would

45
00:02:01,790 --> 00:02:03,439
include at the selected time.

46
00:02:03,879 --> 00:02:06,700
Check trade completion and the visible duty coverage.

47
00:02:07,170 --> 00:02:10,800
A common problem to watch for: A calendar event is not

48
00:02:10,800 --> 00:02:12,540
automatically a duty assignment.

49
00:02:12,870 --> 00:02:16,500
Shift dispatch behavior must be reviewed on Dispatch Settings.

50
00:02:17,149 --> 00:02:18,779
Your practice task is simple.

51
00:02:19,219 --> 00:02:22,569
Schedule a weekend duty crew, change one assignment, and

52
00:02:22,569 --> 00:02:24,370
verify the expected roster.

53
00:02:24,779 --> 00:02:27,500
Track unfilled slots and unresolved trades.

54
00:02:27,849 --> 00:02:31,729
Record the result, who owns the follow-up, and when it should be reviewed.

